NAME
====

**wattle-check** - compile a script without running it

SYNOPSIS
========

**wattle check** [_options_] [_script_]

DESCRIPTION
===========

**wattle check** compiles _script_ without running it and reports compilation
errors. If no script is specified, the command reads source from standard
input. **c** is a synonym for ***check***.

By default, the command reports all errors and exits with status 0. With
**-b**, it stops at the first error and exits with status 1.

OPTIONS
=======

- **-e**, **--lint-error** _level_:
  Set the lint level at which a message stops compilation with an error.
  _level_ is one of ``none``, ``relaxed``, ``normal``, ``strict`` or ``all``,
  written without a leading colon. These names correspond to the numeric
  levels 0, 1, 2, 3 and infinity. A numeric level can also be given directly.
  A message is reported when its level is at most _level_. As a result,
  ``none`` reports no messages and ``all`` reports every message. The default
  is ``none``.

- **-w**, **--lint-warn** _level_:
  Set the lint level at which a message is printed as a warning. _level_ takes
  the same values as for **-e**. The default is ``normal``.

- **-b**, **--bail**:
  Stop at the first error in the source and exit with status 1.

EXAMPLES
========

Check a script for compile errors without running it, stopping at the first:

````
$ wattle check --bail hello.wattle
````

SEE ALSO
========

`wattle(1)`, `wattle-test(1)`
